from (values
('127.890.d.5.C',3,'A mechanic changes three-eighths into a decimal to read a drill-size chart. She is:','["guessing", "ignoring decimals", "converting between fractions and decimals", "skipping math"]'::jsonb,2,'Fractions.','original'),
('127.890.d.5.C',4,'The fraction 3/8 written as a decimal is:','["0.38 even", "0.83", "0.25", "0.375"]'::jsonb,3,'3 divided by 8.','original'),
('127.890.d.5.D',3,'A torque spec reads 120 inch-pounds, but the wrench reads in foot-pounds. She is:','["computing torque value conversions between inch-pounds and foot-pounds", "ignoring units", "guessing", "skipping math"]'::jsonb,0,'Torque convert.','original'),
('127.890.d.5.D',4,'120 inch-pounds of torque equals how many foot-pounds?','["100 foot-pounds", "10 foot-pounds", "1,440 foot-pounds", "12 foot-pounds"]'::jsonb,1,'Divide by 12.','original'),
('127.890.d.5.E',3,'A mechanic divides a cylinder''s volume with the piston at the bottom by the volume with the piston at the top to find the ratio. She is:','["guessing", "ignoring ratio", "computing the compression ratio of a reciprocating engine cylinder", "skipping math"]'::jsonb,2,'Compression ratio.','original'),
('127.890.d.5.E',4,'A cylinder holds 640 cubic centimeters at the bottom of the stroke and 80 at the top. Its compression ratio is:','["7 to 1", "560 to 1", "9 to 1", "8 to 1"]'::jsonb,3,'640/80.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.A',3,'A student explains how electrons move, how magnetism, capacitance, induction and dc and ac relate. She is:','["explaining electron theory, including magnetism, capacitance, induction, direct current and alternating current", "describing seats", "describing tires", "ignoring electricity"]'::jsonb,0,'Electron theory.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.A',4,'In direct current, electrons flow:','["not at all", "in one direction only", "back and forth", "in circles only"]'::jsonb,1,'DC.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.B',3,'A student applies Ohm''s Law, Kirchhoff''s Law and Watt''s Law to a circuit. She is:','["describing seats", "describing tires", "explaining electrical theories and laws", "ignoring laws"]'::jsonb,2,'Laws.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.B',4,'Ohm''s Law says that voltage equals:','["current plus resistance", "resistance minus current", "current over resistance", "current times resistance"]'::jsonb,3,'V=IR.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.C',3,'A student explains how a voltmeter, an ammeter and an ohmmeter are connected to measure a circuit. She is:','["identifying and explaining electrical measurement principles and related tools and procedures", "describing seats", "ignoring meters", "describing tires"]'::jsonb,0,'Measuring.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.C',4,'An ammeter is connected to a circuit:','["not at all", "in series", "in parallel", "across the battery"]'::jsonb,1,'Ammeter series.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.D',3,'A student compares lead-acid, nickel-cadmium and lithium aircraft batteries. She is:','["describing seats", "ignoring batteries", "comparing types of batteries", "describing tires"]'::jsonb,2,'Battery types.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.D',4,'A lead-acid battery stores energy using:','["only air", "only paper", "only water", "lead plates in an electrolyte of sulfuric acid"]'::jsonb,3,'Lead acid.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.E',3,'A student lines up bulbs one after another and then side by side to see how each circuit behaves. She is:','["comparing series circuits and parallel circuits", "ignoring circuits", "describing seats", "describing tires"]'::jsonb,0,'Circuit types.','original'),
('127.890.d.6.E',4,'If one bulb burns out in a parallel circuit, the other bulbs:','["go out", "stay lit", "blow", "dim to zero"]'::jsonb,1,'Parallel.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.A',3,'A mechanic uses a multimeter to test a fuse for continuity and check a switch in the on and off positions. She is:','["ignoring the meter", "painting the meter", "using multimeters to perform circuit continuity tests and test a switch and fuse", "guessing"]'::jsonb,2,'Multimeter.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.A',4,'A good fuse tested with the power off shows:','["a spark", "high voltage", "infinite resistance", "continuity (near zero resistance)"]'::jsonb,3,'Fuse test.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.B',3,'A mechanic reads symbols for a battery, a switch and a resistor on an aircraft wiring diagram. She is:','["interpreting aircraft electrical circuit diagrams and symbols", "describing tires", "ignoring symbols", "describing seats"]'::jsonb,0,'Diagram symbols.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.B',4,'A wiring diagram symbol with a zigzag line usually shows a:','["ground", "resistor", "battery", "switch"]'::jsonb,1,'Zigzag.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.C',3,'A mechanic checks the electrolyte level and terminal condition and cleans the battery box. She is:','["ignoring the battery", "guessing", "inspecting and servicing an aircraft battery", "painting the battery"]'::jsonb,2,'Battery service.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.C',4,'When servicing a lead-acid battery, a mechanic should wear:','["nothing", "a scarf", "sandals", "gloves and eye protection"]'::jsonb,3,'PPE.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.D',3,'A light won''t turn on, so a mechanic tests the power, the switch and the ground one step at a time. She is:','["identifying faults in circuits by using appropriate troubleshooting techniques", "replacing everything", "ignoring the light", "guessing"]'::jsonb,0,'Troubleshooting.','original'),
('127.890.d.7.D',4,'The best troubleshooting method is to:','["replace every part", "check the simplest and most likely causes first", "ignore the circuit", "guess"]'::jsonb,1,'Simple first.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.A',3,'A student explains how lift, weight, thrust and drag balance in level flight. She is:','["describing tires", "ignoring flight", "explaining the theory of flight, including lift, weight, thrust and drag", "describing seats"]'::jsonb,2,'Four forces.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.A',4,'In steady, level flight, lift equals:','["nothing", "thrust", "drag only", "weight"]'::jsonb,3,'Lift equals weight.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.B',3,'A student explains how ailerons, the elevator and the rudder move the aircraft, and what flaps and trim tabs add. She is:','["describing the function and operation of aircraft flight controls and additional aerodynamic devices", "describing tires", "ignoring controls", "describing seats"]'::jsonb,0,'Flight controls.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.B',4,'The elevator controls the aircraft''s:','["yaw", "pitch", "roll", "fuel"]'::jsonb,1,'Elevator.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.C',3,'A student compares how temperature, pressure and humidity change air density on different days. She is:','["describing seats", "describing tires", "analyzing and comparing standard atmospheric factors affecting atmospheric conditions", "ignoring weather"]'::jsonb,2,'Atmosphere.','original'),
('127.890.d.8.C',4,'Hot, humid air makes air density:','["unchanged", "higher", "infinite", "lower"]'::jsonb,3,'Hot air.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.A',3,'A mechanic sets the altimeter to 29.92 at a field 2,000 feet above sea level to find the pressure altitude. She is:','["determining density and pressure altitude", "skipping math", "guessing", "ignoring altitude"]'::jsonb,0,'Pressure altitude.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.A',4,'With a standard altimeter setting of 29.92 at a 2,000-foot field, pressure altitude is about:','["4,000 feet", "2,000 feet", "0 feet", "1,000 feet"]'::jsonb,1,'Standard day.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.B',3,'A student shows air speeding up and dropping in pressure as it moves through a narrow venturi throat. She is:','["describing seats", "describing tires", "identifying changes to pressure and velocity of a fluid as it passes through a venturi", "ignoring flow"]'::jsonb,2,'Venturi.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.B',4,'In the narrow throat of a venturi, the air has:','["no flow", "lower velocity and lower pressure", "higher velocity and higher pressure", "higher velocity and lower pressure"]'::jsonb,3,'Bernoulli.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.C',3,'A mechanic finds pressure by dividing a 600-pound force by a 20-square-inch piston. She is:','["calculating force, area and pressure for a given scenario related to aircraft maintenance", "skipping math", "ignoring units", "guessing"]'::jsonb,0,'Pressure.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.C',4,'A force of 600 pounds on an area of 20 square inches makes a pressure of:','["12 psi", "30 psi", "620 psi", "580 psi"]'::jsonb,1,'600/20.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.D',3,'A student compares total lift of 2,400 pounds to an aircraft weight of 2,000 pounds. She is:','["ignoring lift", "guessing", "calculating the lift of an aircraft and determining if it will climb, descend or maintain altitude", "skipping math"]'::jsonb,2,'Lift vs weight.','original'),
('127.890.d.9.D',4,'If lift is greater than weight, the aircraft will:','["descend", "hold altitude", "stall at once", "climb"]'::jsonb,3,'Lift greater.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.A',3,'A shop weighs an aircraft to find its empty weight and the center of gravity location. They are:','["describing the purpose of weighing an aircraft and determining its center of gravity", "describing seats", "ignoring weight", "describing tires"]'::jsonb,0,'Why weigh.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.A',4,'An aircraft''s center of gravity must stay within limits so that it:','["flies faster", "remains stable and controllable", "looks better", "burns less"]'::jsonb,1,'CG limits.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.B',3,'A mechanic levels the aircraft, drains unusable fuel as the manual says and reads each scale. She is:','["painting scales", "ignoring steps", "explaining the procedures for weighing an aircraft, including general preparation", "guessing"]'::jsonb,2,'Weighing steps.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.B',4,'Before weighing an aircraft, the mechanic should:','["skip leveling", "fill every tank", "add baggage", "level it and set it to the specified fuel and fluid condition"]'::jsonb,3,'Prep.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.C',3,'A student divides total moment by total weight to find where the balance point is. She is:','["explaining the procedures for calculating center of gravity, including arm and moment", "ignoring math", "guessing", "skipping steps"]'::jsonb,0,'CG calc.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.C',4,'The center of gravity location is found by dividing:','["weight by moment", "total moment by total weight", "arm by weight", "moment by arm"]'::jsonb,1,'CG formula.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.D',3,'A student explains why a load shifted too far aft or an empty weight with missing equipment can make flight unsafe. She is:','["describing seats", "describing tires", "explaining adverse loading considerations, proper empty weight configuration and balance", "ignoring loading"]'::jsonb,2,'Adverse loading.','original'),
('127.890.d.10.D',4,'A center of gravity too far aft makes an aircraft:','["more stable", "slower to roll", "easier to land", "less stable and harder to recover from a stall"]'::jsonb,3,'Aft CG.','original'),
('127.890.d.11.A',3,'A mechanic adds 200 pounds at 10 inches and 100 pounds at 40 inches to find the total weight, moment and balance. She is:','["calculating aircraft weight and balance, including equipment changes", "skipping steps", "guessing", "ignoring math"]'::jsonb,0,'Weight and balance.','original'),
('127.890.d.11.A',4,'Items of 200 pounds at 10 inches and 100 pounds at 40 inches give a center of gravity at:','["50 inches", "20 inches", "25 inches", "30 inches"]'::jsonb,1,'6000/300.','original'),
('127.890.d.11.B',3,'A mechanic opens the TCDS and the aircraft''s weight and balance report to find the datum, the limits and the placards. She is:','["ignoring the TCDS", "guessing", "locating datum, weight and balance information, placarding and limitation requirements", "painting placards"]'::jsonb,2,'Locate W&B.','original'),
('127.890.d.11.B',4,'The datum is the:','["heaviest part", "tail only", "fuel gauge", "imaginary vertical line from which all arms are measured"]'::jsonb,3,'Datum.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
